<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salomon of Brittany</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 874</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Salomon was king of Brittany from 857 to 874. A nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nominoe">Nominoe</a>, he murdered his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erispoe">Erispoe</a> and seized power, then fought and negotiated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>, who granted him the Cotentin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avranchin">Avranchin</a> in 867. Under him the Breton kingdom reached its greatest extent. He was murdered in 874 by conspirators among the Breton nobility and was later venerated as a saint.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Salomon, Salaün in Breton, was the son of Riwallon III of Poher, an elder brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nominoe">Nominoe</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>'s defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jengland">Battle of Jengland</a> in 851, the Frankish king made peace with Salomon's cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erispoe">Erispoe</a>, son of Nominoe. In 852 Salomon swore an oath to Charles as his fidelis and received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennes">Rennes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a> and Retz, a third of Brittany, to hold from the king. He was the most powerful noble at Erispoe's court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Probably fearing the loss of his benefices if <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-stammerer">Louis the Stammerer</a> became king at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-mans">Le Mans</a>, Salomon conspired with the otherwise unknown Almarchus to kill <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erispoe">Erispoe</a> and took the Breton throne in 857. He was behind the great revolt of the Frankish nobles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neustria">Neustria</a> against Charles in 858, and a synod at Savonnières in 859 ordered him in vain to keep his oath and resume Brittany's tribute. In 862 he hired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-the-strong">Robert the Strong</a>. Charles marched on Brittany in 863 but made peace near Entrammes, recognising western Anjou as Breton and granting Salomon the lay abbacy of Saint-Aubin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angers">Angers</a> in return for tribute.</p><p>War resumed in 865, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bretons">Bretons</a> ravaged the land around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-mans">Le Mans</a>, and in 866 Robert was killed at the Battle of Brissarthe. In August 867 Salomon's son-in-law Pascweten made peace with Charles at Compiègne, and the peace held for the rest of Salomon's life. In 868 Charles sent him regalia, among them a golden jewelled crown, and probably stood godfather to his son Wigo. Salomon also tried to persuade Pope Nicholas I to send the pallium to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Dol, which would have given the Breton sees an archbishop of their own instead of the Archdiocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 874 Pascweten, Wrhwant and Wigo, son of Riwallon, Count of Cornouaille, conspired against Salomon and killed him. The conspirators soon fell out, and a civil war followed until 876. Popular tradition in Brittany later venerated him as Saint Salomon and a martyr, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> honours him on 25 June.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Salomon of Brittany” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Brittany, the Armorican peninsula settled by migrants from south-western Britain in the fifth and sixth centuries, had formed a kingdom under Nominoe, Erispoe and <b>Salomon</b> in the ninth century, when its rulers fought the Carolingians and won the counties of Rennes and Nantes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Salomon</b>, who killed Erispoe and took the throne in 857, gained the Cotentin and Avranchin in 867.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-brittany">Kingdom of Brittany</a></li></ul></section>
